  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This specimen is a good fit if the hashed unit is the canonical DAG-CBOR envelope [pCID, payload]: Bob can recover object interpretation from pCID inside the bytes instead of from a filename or CID codec alone. It scores lower because the draft does not fully specify bare hashed payload blobs, the concrete pointer-versus-raw payload protocols, or the long-term archival story for pCID meanings.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID binds payload interpretation to transported bytes instead of local filenames.",
      "The no-speculative-parsing rule keeps raw bytes and typed object semantics cleanly separated.",
      "Unknown-pCID opaque store-and-forward behavior supports sparse-knowledge interoperability and evolution."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The answer is strongest only when the addressed object is the envelope itself; the draft is less clear for bare referenced blobs.",
      "Pointer-object versus raw-chunk meaning is delegated to payload protocols not defined here.",
      "Draft status, no freeze, and no minted pCID weaken 100-year durability claims."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If systems hash or exchange payload bytes outside the envelope, raw-versus-pointer ambiguity can return.",
      "Long-term audit depends on durable archival of pCID-linked specs without a central registry.",
      "Nested wrapper signatures can complicate straightforward type inspection and policy checks."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Are all type-sensitive CAS objects required to be grid envelopes, or are some raw blobs first-class?",
      "How are pCID specs discovered and archived for offline auditors over decades?",
      "What concrete payload conventions separate pointer objects from raw chunks, and how are mismatches detected?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
